            <div xml:id="d557">

                <pb facs="0532" n="524" sameAs="frus:frus1940v04/pg524"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94112/368: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Hankow</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Spiker</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Hankow</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-05-30T10:00:00+08:00">May 30, 1940—10
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received May 30—4:15 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>42. Reference my 34, April 30, 3 p.m. Japanese military liaison officer here has
                    stated that restrictions on travel on the Yangtse by third power nationals have
                    been lifted. Japanese Consulate General <pb facs="0533" n="525" xml:id="pg525"/>
                    has today indicated further steps are being taken in relation to the
                    transportation to Shanghai of Americans now awaiting passage.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Chungking, Peiping and Shanghai.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Spiker</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
